      <li>
      Validate OLM Operator compatibility with the target y-stream version by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csv -A"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>All Operators must show <code>Succeeded</code> status. Investigate any Operators in a failed state before proceeding.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Review Operator subscription channels to ensure they support the target OpenShift Container Platform version by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get subscription -A -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{\"\\t\"}{.spec.channel}{\"\\n\"}{end}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Update Operator subscriptions if needed before the cluster update.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a RHACM policy to pause worker nodes:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-pause-worker-nodes\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: pause-worker-mcp\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: machineconfiguration.openshift.io/v1\n            kind: MachineConfigPool\n            metadata:\n              name: worker\n            spec:\n              paused: true"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a RHACM policy for the y-stream update:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-upgrade-4.21-policy\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: upgrade-cluster-version\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\n            kind: ClusterVersion\n            metadata:\n              name: version\n            spec:\n              channel: stable-4.21\n              desiredUpdate:\n                version: 4.21.0"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Replace <code>4.21.0</code> with your target y-stream version. Both policies are set to <code>inform</code> so they do not immediately push changes. The <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> custom resource (CR) enforces these policies at the scheduled update time.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the policies to RHACM by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-pause-worker-policy.yaml\n$ oc apply -f core-upgrade-4.21-policy.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify the policies and placement resources were created by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get policy -n openshift-upgrade-policies\n$ oc get placementbinding -n openshift-upgrade-policies\n$ oc get placement -n openshift-upgrade-policies"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If no <code>PlacementBinding</code> or <code>Placement</code> exists, the policies will not apply to any clusters. Create appropriate placement resources for your target clusters.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR for the y-stream update:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: core-ystream-upgrade-4.21\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - spoke1\n  managedPolicies:\n  - core-pause-worker-nodes\n  - core-upgrade-4.21-policy\n  enable: false\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1\n    timeout: 120\n  actions:\n    beforeEnable:\n      addClusterLabels:\n        upgrade-in-progress: \"true\"\n    afterCompletion:\n      addClusterLabels:\n        upgraded-to: \"4.21\"\n        control-plane-upgraded: \"true\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>For canary deployments, use a single cluster first before updating additional clusters.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply and enable the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR to start the control plane update by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-ystream-cgu.yaml\n$ oc patch cgu core-ystream-upgrade-4.21 \\\n  -n openshift-upgrade-policies \\\n  --type merge \\\n  -p '{\"spec\":{\"enable\":true}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor the control plane update progress by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu core-ystream-upgrade-4.21 -n openshift-upgrade-policies -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get co"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Cluster Operators update sequentially during the y-stream update. The control plane update typically takes 60 to 120 minutes.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      After the control plane update completes, verify control plane health by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/master=\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get co | grep -v \"True.*False.*False\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>All control plane nodes and Operators must be healthy before proceeding.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create an OLM Operator update policy that updates Operator subscription channels to versions compatible with the new y-stream:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-upgrade-olm-4.21\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: upgrade-olm-operators\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\n            kind: Subscription\n            metadata:\n              name: sriov-network-operator-subscription\n              namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\n            spec:\n              channel: stable-4.21\n            status:\n              state: AtLatestKnown"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>You can include multiple Operator subscriptions in the same policy. For more details about creating OLM update policies, see "Coordinating OLM Operator updates with cluster updates".</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR to apply the OLM Operator update policy:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: core-olm-upgrade-4.21\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - spoke1\n  managedPolicies:\n  - core-upgrade-olm-4.21\n  enable: true\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1\n    timeout: 60"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the OLM Operator update policy and <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-olm-upgrade-policy.yaml\n$ oc apply -f core-olm-cgu.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor the OLM Operator update and verify that worker nodes remain at the previous version by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu core-olm-upgrade-4.21 -n openshift-upgrade-policies -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Worker nodes must still show the previous kubelet version.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a policy to unpause worker nodes:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-unpause-worker-nodes\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: unpause-worker-mcp\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: machineconfiguration.openshift.io/v1\n            kind: MachineConfigPool\n            metadata:\n              name: worker\n            spec:\n              paused: false"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR to unpause workers:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: core-unpause-workers-4.21\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - spoke1\n  managedPolicies:\n  - core-unpause-worker-nodes\n  enable: false\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply and enable the unpause <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-unpause-workers-cgu.yaml\n$ oc patch cgu core-unpause-workers-4.21 \\\n  -n openshift-upgrade-policies \\\n  --type merge \\\n  -p '{\"spec\":{\"enable\":true}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor worker node updates by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get mcp worker -w"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Worker nodes update in a rolling fashion. The process typically takes 30 to 60 minutes depending on the number of worker nodes.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify all nodes are at the target y-stream version by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify all cluster Operators are healthy by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get co"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify workload health and CNF-specific resources by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get performanceprofile\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get ptpconfig -n openshift-ptp\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get sriovnetworknodepolicy -n openshift-sriov-network-operator"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Run smoke tests for critical workloads.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>If API deprecation warnings appear, update manifests to use new API versions before updating.</li>
      <li>If Operator compatibility issues occur, update Operator subscriptions to channels that support the target y-stream.</li>
      <li>If worker node updates get stuck, check <code>MachineConfigPool</code> resource status and node logs.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="core-cluster-upgrade-y-stream-sequential_core-cluster-upgrades-y-stream" class="group scroll-mt-20">Updating through sequential y-stream releases<a href="#core-cluster-upgrade-y-stream-sequential_core-cluster-upgrades-y-stream" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can update clusters through multiple sequential y-stream releases when you need to move from an older version to a newer version that is more than one minor release away. You must update through each intermediate y-stream version because skipping versions is not supported.</p>
      <p>For example, to update from 4.20 to 4.22, you must first update from 4.20 to 4.21, and then update from 4.21 to 4.22.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The following procedure uses <code>spoke1</code> as an example cluster name. Replace it with your actual cluster name throughout.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>When planning to update clusters through multiple sequential y-stream releases, allow at least 24 hours, and up to 48 hours, between y-stream updates to monitor cluster stability.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have completed the pre-update health check successfully.</li>
      <li>TALM is installed and configured on the Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) hub cluster.</li>
      <li>You have access to the target clusters with cluster-admin privileges.</li>
      <li>The <code>oc</code> CLI tool is installed and configured for the target clusters.</li>
      <li>All intermediate y-stream releases are available, such as both 4.21 and 4.22 when updating from 4.20 to 4.22.</li>
      <li>You have reviewed the release notes for each intermediate y-stream version for API deprecations and breaking changes.</li>
      <li>You have planned maintenance windows for each y-stream update.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Determine the complete update path:
      <p>Identify your current version and target version, then map out the required intermediate versions.</p>
      <p><strong>Example update paths</strong></p>
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>Current version</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Update path to 4.22</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell>4.20.20</TableCell>
      <TableCell>4.20.20 → 4.21.latest → 4.22.0</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell>4.19.10</TableCell>
      <TableCell>4.19.10 → 4.20.latest → 4.21.latest → 4.22.0</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      </li>
      <li>
      Update to the first intermediate y-stream version:
      <p>Follow the procedure in "Updating clusters through y-stream releases" to update to the first intermediate version.</p>
      <p>For example, update from 4.20.20 to 4.21.latest.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify the first y-stream update completed successfully by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Verify all cluster Operators are healthy by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get co | grep -v \"True.*False.*False\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>All cluster Operators must show healthy status before proceeding.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Wait at least 24 hours, and up to 48 hours, between y-stream updates:
      <p>Allow time to monitor cluster stability after the first update. Extend this period for large production deployments or if any anomalies are observed.</p>
      <p>During this wait period:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Monitor workload health and performance.</li>
      <li>Check for unexpected behavior or errors.</li>
      <li>Run smoke tests for critical cloud-native network function (CNF) workloads.</li>
      <li>Verify all custom resources specific to your deployment are functioning correctly.</li>
      <li>Review release notes for the next y-stream version for additional API deprecations or configuration changes.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Update OLM Operator subscriptions if needed:
      <p>Some Operators might require intermediate channel updates when updating through multiple y-streams. Verify the Operator status by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get csv -A"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Verify all Operators are at versions compatible with your current cluster version before proceeding.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create RHACM policies for the second y-stream update:
      <p>Update the cluster version policy to target the next y-stream:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-upgrade-4.22-policy\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: upgrade-cluster-version\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\n            kind: ClusterVersion\n            metadata:\n              name: version\n            spec:\n              channel: stable-4.22\n              desiredUpdate:\n                version: 4.22.0"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the updated policy by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-upgrade-4.22-policy.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a new <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> custom resource (CR) for the second y-stream:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: core-ystream-upgrade-4.22\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - spoke1\n  managedPolicies:\n  - core-pause-worker-nodes\n  - core-upgrade-4.22-policy\n  enable: false\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 1\n    timeout: 120"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply and enable the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-ystream-upgrade-4.22-cgu.yaml\n$ oc patch cgu core-ystream-upgrade-4.22 \\\n  -n openshift-upgrade-policies \\\n  --type merge \\\n  -p '{\"spec\":{\"enable\":true}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor the second y-stream update and verify completion by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu core-ystream-upgrade-4.22 -n openshift-upgrade-policies -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get co"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Update Operators for the second y-stream by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get csv -A | grep -v Succeeded"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Update Operator channels as needed for the new cluster version.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Unpause worker nodes and complete the second y-stream update:
      <p>Follow the worker node unpause procedure from "Pausing and unpausing worker nodes by using TALM".</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Repeat steps 2-12 for any additional intermediate y-stream versions:
      <p>If updating through more than two y-streams, repeat the process for each intermediate version.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the final cluster version by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.desired.version}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Run comprehensive health checks:
      <p>Follow the procedure in "Performing health checks before cluster updates".</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify workload functionality after all updates:
      <p>Run smoke tests and validate that all CNF workloads operate correctly at the final version.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>If the cluster becomes degraded after the first y-stream update, resolve all issues before proceeding to the second y-stream.</li>
      <li>If Operator versions are incompatible with an intermediate y-stream, update the Operator subscription before updating the cluster.</li>
      <li>If configuration changes are needed between y-streams, apply those changes during the wait period before proceeding.</li>
      </ul>
